This is a known issue. Android deprecated `SOFT_INPUT_ADJUST_RESIZE` (API 30+), and .NET 10 MAUI no longer maps `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize` to it reliably.

## Fix: Use Android WindowInsets

**1. Set `adjustResize` on the Activity** (`Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs`):

```csharp
[Activity(Theme = "@style/Maui.SplashTheme",
          MainLauncher = true,
          WindowSoftInputMode = SoftInput.AdjustResize,
          ConfigurationChanges = /* ... */)]
public class MainActivity : MauiAppCompatActivity { }
```

**2. If that alone isn't enough** (common with edge-to-edge / API 35+), add an inset listener in `MainActivity`:

```csharp
protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
{
    base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);

    // Ensure the content area resizes for the keyboard
    AndroidX.Core.View.ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(
        Window!.DecorView.FindViewById(Android.Resource.Id.Content)!,
        new KeyboardInsetsListener());
}

private class KeyboardInsetsListener : Java.Lang.Object,
    AndroidX.Core.View.IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
{
    public AndroidX.Core.View.WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(
        Android.Views.View v,
        AndroidX.Core.View.WindowInsetsCompat insets)
    {
        var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(
            AndroidX.Core.View.WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
        var systemBarInsets = insets.GetInsets(
            AndroidX.Core.View.WindowInsetsCompat.Type.SystemBars());

        // Pad bottom by whichever is larger: keyboard or nav bar
        v.SetPadding(
            systemBarInsets.Left, systemBarInsets.Top,
            systemBarInsets.Right,
            Math.Max(imeInsets.Bottom, systemBarInsets.Bottom));

        return insets;
    }
}
```

**3. For iOS**, it generally works out of the box. If not, set `KeyboardAutoManagerScroll` in `MauiProgram.cs`:

```csharp
builder.ConfigureMauiHandlers(h =>
{
#if IOS
    Microsoft.Maui.Platform.KeyboardAutoManagerScroll.Connect = true;
#endif
});
```

## Layout tip

Use a `Grid` with your chat list in a star-sized row and the input bar in an `Auto` row at the bottom — this lets the resize push the input up naturally:

```xml
<Grid RowDefinitions="*, Auto">
    <CollectionView Grid.Row="0" ... />
    <Entry Grid.Row="1" Placeholder="Message..." />
</Grid>
```

The key takeaway: `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize` mapped to a deprecated Android API. The modern fix is handling `WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime()` directly.
